{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Decatur homes have a downspout problem they don't even know about. Older homes in the Bank Street and Albany areas often have original downspouts that are undersized by modern standards. Newer construction frequently uses builder-grade downspouts that meet minimum code but can't handle heavy storms.</p>

<p>The Tennessee River's influence on Decatur makes proper downspouts even more critical. The river raises local water tables, meaning the ground is often already saturated when rain falls. Water that pools near your foundation can't drain into already-saturated soil — it just sits there, finding its way into your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>We see this constantly throughout Decatur: homes with "working" gutter systems that are actually funneling thousands of gallons of water directly toward the foundation every year. The downspouts are too small, there aren't enough of them, or they dump water just inches from the foundation wall.</p>

<p>The fix is usually straightforward: properly sized downspouts, correctly positioned, with extensions or underground drains that move water far from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three essential components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective water dispersal.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and Decatur's rainfall intensity. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch — they handle heavy storms without backing up.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows to corners and low points in your gutter system. Poorly placed downspouts create dead zones where water pools instead of draining.</p>

<p>Water dispersal means getting that water far from your foundation. Basic splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. For Decatur's high water table conditions, underground drains that route water 10+ feet away provide the best protection.</p>
{% endset %}

